{"id":"authorized-against-filled","title":"Positions authorized against positions filled","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"How many positions does the Annual Salary Ordinance authorize in each department, how many full-time equivalents were actually paid on permanent, exempt or provisional appointments, and where are the largest gaps?","finding":"The Annual Salary Ordinance authorized 38,470 positions in job classes for fiscal 2026; the payroll shows 34,060 full-time equivalents paid on permanent civil-service, permanent exempt and provisional appointments, 11.5% fewer. The ordinance carries a further 907 full-time equivalents on lines titled “Temporary - Miscellaneous” and “Temporary - Nurses” (wording normalised from the scans), against 2,222 actually paid on temporary and other appointments. The largest gaps are in Public Health (1,164 of 8,301), Municipal Transportation Agency (928 of 6,416) and Public Utilities Commission (580 of 2,798). 84 class-and-department lines carried at least one authorized position in each of the five years to 2026 with nobody paid in them (147 positions in 2026), and 20 were paid well above their authorized count.","how":"Position lists of the 12 enacted Annual Salary Ordinances on disk, each of which covers two fiscal years; the first-year figure of the later ordinance is taken as the enacted count for a year. Paid full-time equivalents are hours paid divided by the paid hours of a full-time year in that fiscal year, read off the file (2,080 in most years, 2,160 in fiscal 2026), from the City payroll, fiscal 2017 to 2026, for rows whose type of appointment is permanent civil service, permanent exempt or provisional; temporary exempt and other rows are shown separately, beside the ordinance’s own lines for temporary staff, which are not job classes and are left out of the position count. Ordinance department codes are matched to the payroll’s. “Never filled” counts a class in a department authorized at one position or more in each of the last five years with no hours paid in it there in any of them; “paid above” counts a class paid at least three full-time equivalents and half as much again above its authorized count. Ranked by the gap between authorized and paid.","limits":"The ordinances whose first year is fiscal 2016 and 2019 are scans whose department totals reconcile only in part, so those years’ counts are approximate. The ordinances print no flags for new, substituted or deleted positions, and mid-year amendments are not on disk. A gap is not waste in itself: budgets assume some posts stay vacant, a post can be held by someone paid in a lower or different class while it is being filled, and hours fall short of 2,080 for anyone who started or left during the year. Those same substitutions are why a class can be paid above its count. Spending against filled positions by department is in the screen “Spending up, filled positions down” under waivers and exceptions and is not repeated here.","columns":[{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"authorized","t":"Positions authorized, fiscal 2026","type":"int"},{"k":"paidPermanent","t":"Paid, permanent, exempt and provisional (full-time equivalents)","type":"int"},{"k":"tempLines","t":"Ordinance lines for temporary staff (full-time equivalents)","type":"int"},{"k":"paidAll","t":"Paid on temporary and other appointments","type":"int"},{"k":"gap","t":"Authorized but not paid","type":"int"},{"k":"gapShare","t":"Share of authorized","type":"pct"},{"k":"neverFilled","t":"Class lines authorized five years, never paid","type":"int"},{"k":"neverFilledPositions","t":"Positions on those lines","type":"int"},{"k":"overfilled","t":"Class lines paid above the count","type":"int"},{"k":"overPositions","t":"Full-time equivalents above the count","type":"int"}],"rows":[{"department":"Public Health","authorized":8301,"paidPermanent":7138,"paidAll":563,"tempLines":154,"gap":1164,"gapShare":0.1402,"neverFilled":0,"neverFilledPositions":0,"overfilled":1,"